# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules. This file is copied verbatim to the site root
# by VitePress (everything under platform/public/ lands in .vitepress/dist/).
#
# It has no effect under `pnpm dev` — the VitePress dev server does not read it.
# Test it with `pnpm pages:dev`, which serves the built output through the same
# Pages runtime that production uses.
#
# Syntax: <from> <to> <status>. `:name` captures one path segment and is
# substituted on the right. First matching rule wins, so put specific rules
# above general ones. Cloudflare Pages allows 2,000 static and 100 dynamic
# rules. Every numbered challenge takes one static rule per short-link form, 4
# in all, so a range holds at most 500 numbered challenges. The slug links
# take 4 dynamic rules, and old /zh-TW/ addresses take one more dynamic rule
# for each top-level folder of pages and one static rule for each top-level
# page and for /zh-TW/ itself.
#
# `platform/public/_redirects` is generated from the challenges; do not edit it
# by hand. `pnpm create:challenge` and the pre-commit hook regenerate it when
# challenges change. Commit it together with them: GitHub Pages does not read
# it, but CI checks that it matches the challenges. If CI reports it stale, run
# `pnpm redirects:generate` and commit the result.
#
# `pnpm build` rewrites it too. The CI check is
# tests/unit/site/redirects-generated.test.ts.

# Numbered short links, 4 per challenge. A number is assigned once and never
# reused, so a link printed on a handout keeps pointing at the same challenge
# even after the challenge is renamed. The copy control gives each locale's
# own form, /en/c/NNN and /zh-TW/c/NNN; /c/NNN, which opens the primary
# locale's page, and /zh/c/NNN are kept so links already handed out keep
# opening. A locale's rules open the Traditional Chinese page, the primary
# locale's, while the challenge has no page in that locale, or, for a
# challenge without a primary-locale page, the first locale's page that
# exists.
#
# These come first on purpose: Cloudflare takes the first match and `/c/:slug`
# matches any single segment, a number included. A numeric rule placed after it
# would never run, and nothing about that would be visible at deploy time.
/c/001              /challenge/door-is-open/          302
/en/c/001           /en/challenge/door-is-open/       302
/zh/c/001           /challenge/door-is-open/          302
/zh-TW/c/001        /challenge/door-is-open/          302
/c/002              /challenge/staff-only/            302
/en/c/002           /en/challenge/staff-only/         302
/zh/c/002           /challenge/staff-only/            302
/zh-TW/c/002        /challenge/staff-only/            302
/c/008              /challenge/just-a-preview/        302
/en/c/008           /en/challenge/just-a-preview/     302
/zh/c/008           /challenge/just-a-preview/        302
/zh-TW/c/008        /challenge/just-a-preview/        302
/c/010              /challenge/selected-excerpts/     302
/en/c/010           /en/challenge/selected-excerpts/  302
/zh/c/010           /challenge/selected-excerpts/     302
/zh-TW/c/010        /challenge/selected-excerpts/     302

# Slug short links for classroom use — students can be given /en/c/<slug> or
# /zh-TW/c/<slug> to type or paste rather than the full /challenge/<slug>/ path;
# /c/<slug> and /zh/c/<slug> keep working. Parameterised, so they follow a
# rename; the numbered rules above are what survives one.
/c/:slug            /challenge/:slug/                 302
/en/c/:slug         /en/challenge/:slug/              302
/zh/c/:slug         /challenge/:slug/                 302
/zh-TW/c/:slug      /challenge/:slug/                 302

# Addresses under /zh-TW/ from when Traditional Chinese was a translation.
# This range's primary locale is Traditional Chinese, so its pages are at the
# root: /zh-TW/ goes to /, each top-level page /zh-TW/<name> goes to /<name>,
# and each top-level folder of pages /zh-TW/<folder>/<path> goes to
# /<folder>/<path>. Every target begins with a fixed name, never with text
# from the request. The folder c is left out: /zh-TW/c/ belongs to the short
# links above. An address no rule covers is handled by the not-found page.
/zh-TW/             /                                 302
/zh-TW/challenge/*  /challenge/:splat                 302
/zh-TW/challenges   /challenges                       302
/zh-TW/guide/*      /guide/:splat                     302
